{"page_content": "ACME/Flame Design Final Report (2023) \n \n6 April 2023 \n23 \n \n \n \nThe maximum limit of 999 seconds for the valve safety timers was used for all ACME testing. \n \nAnother constraint is the available time in a day, where the console personnel and ISS resources, \nsuch as the video downlink, are planned for a limited number of hours.  When necessary for the \nresearch, NASA does have continuous 24-hour/day operations for some non-CIR experiments.  \nBut as discussed, the ACME tests are typically two minutes or less in duration, so the testing is \nalmost always limited to the planned durations. \n \nData limits are yet another constraint, where the on-orbit hardware can store a limited amount \nof data.  More importantly, 127,000 MB is the most that can be reasonably managed in a two-\nshift transfer and downlink day.  Therefore, tests days are limited to this amount of data, where \nimage data from select cameras is often archived at reduced rates to mitigate this challenge.  \nNonetheless, the limit sometimes forces decisions to abandon recorded images. \n \nThis detailed description of operations and hardware provides the context under which the ACME \nexperiments are conducted.  \u2026 \n \nTable 1 is a summary log of the flames ignited for Flame Design during the two rounds of testing, \nincluding a limited overview of the test conditions.  They were copied from this report\u2019s Appendix \nC. 30-Day Operational Accomplishments Report for ISS Increment 66 which comprehensively \nreflects all Flame Design testing on the ISS.", "metadata": {"source": "PSI-10_Reports_FinalRpt_ACMEFlameDesign.pdf", "page": 23}}